# Common values shared for all charts
release_name: datahub-local-ai

# Delivery knobs, consumed by templates/ensembles.yaml and substituted into each
# persona's system prompt. Deliberately kept out of the tree below: everything
# under sympozium_ensembles is merged into the Ensemble spec, and the CRD webhook
# decodes strictly — an unknown key is rejected outright ("unknown field
# spec.verbosity"), not pruned. Chart-only knobs need their own tree.
#
# Keys are the ensemble directory name, with optional per-persona overrides.
#   channel    where send_channel_message posts. No CRD field carries a
#              destination, so this only ever reaches the agent as prompt text —
#              substituted into the CHANNEL token and, from there, into the
#              tool's `chatId` argument. See prompts/delivery/*.md: `chatId` is
#              the destination and `channel` is the transport ("slack"), and the
#              first version of these prompts had them the wrong way round,
#              which is why nothing was ever delivered.
#
#              Note the token is named without its braces here on purpose. This
#              file is a .gotmpl: helmfile renders it as a Go template before
#              Helm sees it, comments included, so a literal brace pair is a
#              template action and an undefined function — which is exactly how
#              this line broke the ArgoCD CMP once.
#   verbosity  quiet | normal | verbose  -> prompts/delivery/<level>.md
#   notify     always | onChange | never -> prompts/notify/<level>.md
#
# Channel names rather than C0… ids, so this file stays readable. Slack accepts
# a name for chat.postMessage, but it is the legacy form and breaks silently on
# a rename — swap in the ids if these channels start moving. The bot has to be a
# member of every channel named here.
#
# Delivery needs BOTH halves and there is no way to split them. An entry here is
# what tells the agent where to post; the persona's `channels: [slack]` is what
# lets the message leave the pod at all. `send_channel_message` is registered even
# on an unbound persona and still answers, but the ipc-bridge then drops it —
# "Dropping outbound message to channel not configured on this agent" — and the
# run reports Succeeded having posted nothing. Verified with a probe on
# 2026-08-23; scripts/validate.py fails either half missing.
#
# That coupling is why every report arrives five times. A binding also deploys a
# channel sidecar, and every slack sidecar in the namespace delivers every
# instance's outbound message — it filters on the transport in `data.channel` and
# never on the `metadata.instanceName` it is handed. Five delivering personas
# means five sidecars means five identical copies, in the same second. Nothing in
# this file or in projects/ can separate them: unbinding four personas to leave
# one sidecar carrying the ensemble was tried and silences those four completely.
# The fix is one line upstream: filter on metadata.instanceName in the sidecar.
#
# How a report reaches its channel, for personas with deliveryMode: hook.
#
# The hook is a container the controller runs in a follow-up Job after the agent
# finishes: it gets the report in AGENT_RESULT and posts it with one
# chat.postMessage call. Nothing touches the shared event bus, so the report
# arrives exactly once however many personas are bound -- which is the whole
# reason this exists. See ../README.md for the fan-out it replaces.
#
# `secret` is the same Secret channelConfigs points at, read by reference so no
# token appears in this chart. Egress to slack.com works because the hook pod is
# not labelled sympozium.ai/role=agent and so is not selected by
# sympozium-agent-deny-all -- verified against api.test from inside the pod.
#
# Pin `image` to a digest if you want the hook reproducible; a tag is enough
# here, and curl is the whole dependency.
sympozium_delivery_hook:
  image: curlimages/curl:8.11.1
  secret: mcp-slack-token
  tokenKey: SLACK_BOT_TOKEN

sympozium_delivery:
  homelab-ops:
    # The daily and weekly personas report here every run — hardware, the
    # databases, the weekly cleanup. Scan-later reading, so this is the channel
    # whose notifications can be off.
    channel: "#monitoring-ai-health"

    # Split by what a reader would do about it, not by which agent produced it:
    # a channel is really one notification setting. Keeping the two frequent
    # personas out of -health is what lets -alerts keep notifications on without
    # the daily hardware report training you to mute it.
    # Only the destination is per-persona now. deliveryMode defaults to hook, so
    # neither of these says so; a persona that ever needs the sidecar path opts
    # out with `deliveryMode: tool` and pays a duplicate copy per bound persona.
    #
    # `verbosity` and `notify` are deliberately absent everywhere. A hook posts
    # the run's own result unconditionally, so neither knob can do anything -
    # they described how the model should call the posting tool and when to stay
    # quiet, and there is no longer a call and no suppression. Leaving them set
    # would have claimed behaviour that does not happen, so validate.py rejects
    # them on a hook-mode persona. The cost is real and worth stating: every
    # report now arrives every run, so -alerts gets one every 30 minutes where
    # onChange used to keep it near-silent. Stretch schedule.interval if that is
    # too much; do not reach for notify.
    personas:
      sre-sentinel:
        channel: "#monitoring-ai-alerts"
      gitops-auditor:
        channel: "#monitoring-ai-drift"

  # homelab-reviewer is absent on purpose: it is bound to no channel, and its
  # DO NOT MERGE comment on the pull request is the alert. See
  # projects/homelab-reviewer/README.md#notification.

# Which agents also answer over HTTP, so a run can be started off-schedule to
# test one. Chart-only, like sympozium_delivery above: enabling it appends the
# `web-endpoint` SkillPack to the persona, and the controller responds by
# deploying a web-proxy Deployment and a ClusterIP Service beside the agent. The
# CRD's own `spec.webEndpoint` field is deprecated in favour of the skill, so it
# is deliberately not set anywhere.
#
# The proxy turns one HTTP request into one AgentRun, against the same prompts,
# skills, MCP servers and tool policy the schedule uses — which is the point: a
# test that skipped any of those would not be testing the agent that runs at
# 04:30. It costs a Deployment per persona and, on each request, a slot on the
# one GPU.
#
#   POST /v1/chat/completions   OpenAI-compatible
#   POST /v1/mcp                MCP
#   GET  /healthz               the only route needing no Authorization header
#
# No `hostname` is set, so no HTTPRoute is created and the Service stays
# ClusterIP: reachable by `kubectl port-forward`, and by nothing outside the
# cluster. See ../README.md#testing-an-agent-over-http for the key and a call.
#
# `enabled` is the master switch, and it is OFF because an endpoint does not sit
# beside a persona's schedule — it replaces it. A serving AgentRun makes the
# schedule controller skip every tick for that agent ("Skipping trigger —
# instance has a serving AgentRun"), silently: the SympoziumSchedule stays
# Active, no run fails, nothing is emitted. Two ticks were lost that way on
# 2026-08-23 before anything noticed. See ../README.md#the-endpoint-replaces-the-schedule--it-does-not-sit-beside-it.
#
# So this is a switch to flip for the length of a test and flip back, not a
# standing configuration — which is exactly why it is one master switch rather
# than only the per-ensemble keys: turning the surface on and off again must not
# mean editing, and then having to remember to restore, the per-agent decisions
# below. For a test that does not cost a schedule, apply an AgentRun by hand
# instead; the README has the manifest.
#
# Under `ensembles:`, keys are the ensemble directory name, with optional
# per-persona overrides under `personas:`, exactly as sympozium_delivery does.
sympozium_web_endpoint:
  enabled: false

  ensembles:
    homelab-ops:
      enabled: true
      requestsPerMinute: 60
      burstSize: 10

    homelab-reviewer:
      # Off, and not for symmetry: renovate-reviewer is the one persona holding
      # a write tool (github_add_issue_comment), and what it writes lands on a
      # real pull request. It is also the one ensemble deliberately bound to no
      # channel for the same reason. Turn this on for the length of a test and
      # back off afterwards, rather than leaving a trigger sitting in front of
      # it.
      enabled: false

# Per-cluster knobs merged into each Ensemble's spec, keyed by the directory name
# under projects/. Everything describing *what an agent is* — prompts, skills,
# schedules, tool policy — lives in projects/ and is read from there by
# templates/ensembles.yaml at render time. Only settings that could legitimately
# differ between clusters belong here.
sympozium_ensembles:
  homelab-ops:
    # Ensembles ship disabled by default in the CRD ("catalog-only"). All six
    # agents are wanted here, so both ensembles are switched on explicitly.
    enabled: true

    # Channel type -> the Secret holding its credentials. The controller reads
    # this to set ConfigRef on every generated Agent whose agentConfig lists the
    # type in `channels`, so a persona bound to a type with no entry here binds
    # to nothing at all — scripts/validate.py cross-checks the two.
    #
    # A secret name is a property of the cluster, not of the agent, which is why
    # it lives here and is rejected in ensemble.yaml. mcp-slack-token is
    # projected into `automation` by datahub-local-secrets and carries
    # SLACK_BOT_TOKEN (outbound chat:write) and SLACK_APP_TOKEN (Socket Mode,
    # the inbound half). Until it appeared the only Slack secret in this cluster
    # was `slack-auth` in `monitoring`, which is why nothing here could notify.
    channelConfigs:
      slack: mcp-slack-token

    # Cluster-local Ollama, deployed by datahub-local-core
    # (releases/data/values/ollama.yaml.gotmpl). No credentials: provider
    # "ollama" needs none, which is why no authRefs secret appears anywhere in
    # this chart. The Sympozium NetworkPolicy already allows egress on 11434
    # (extraEgressPorts in core's sympozium values), and Ollama serves an
    # OpenAI-compatible API under /v1.
    baseURL: http://datahub-local-core-data-ollama.data.svc:11434/v1

    # permissive, deliberately — see ../../README.md#why-the-permissive-policy.
    # The built-in restrictive and network-isolated policies both set
    # networkPolicy.denyAll, which would cut the agents off from Ollama and
    # from every MCP server. Restriction is enforced per-agent instead, by the
    # toolPolicy allowlists and the MCP toolsDeny lists in projects/.
    policyRef: permissive

  homelab-reviewer:
    enabled: true
    baseURL: http://datahub-local-core-data-ollama.data.svc:11434/v1
    policyRef: permissive
